+        <div class="desk-note"><span class="small-number">TRY THIS / 01</span><p>Pin a baseline. Increase R. Does the filter follow each fix as closely?</p><p class="hint">Every fix is identical for A and B. Only their assumptions differ.</p></div>
+      </aside>
+    </div>
+    <section class="results panel" aria-labelledby="results-title">
+      <div class="panel-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / PAIRED OBSERVATIONS</p><h2 id="results-title">Same ocean. Two beliefs.</h2></div><span id="result-stage" class="tag">CUMULATIVE · 0 s</span></div>
+      <div class="results-layout"><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Depth RMSE up to the inspected time, in meters. Lower means less error.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Estimator</th><th scope="col">RMSE / m</th><th scope="col">Truth in 95% band</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="b-row"><th scope="row">B / active filter</th><td id="rmse-b">—</td><td id="coverage-b">—</td></tr><tr class="a-row"><th scope="row">A / pinned filter</th><td id="rmse-a">—</td><td id="coverage-a">—</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Raw depth fixes</th><td id="rmse-raw">—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dead reckoning</th><td id="rmse-dead">—</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="hint" id="metric-note">Filter and dead reckoning use every 0.25 s step. Raw RMSE uses available fixes only.</p></div>
+      <div class="diagnosis"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD DIAGNOSIS</p><h3 id="diagnosis-title">Ready for descent.</h3><p id="diagnosis-text">Press Play, step one prediction, or go straight to the mission summary.</p><div class="status-line"><span id="fix-status">Initial state</span><span id="fix-count">0 fixes</span></div></div></div>
+      <p id="summary" class="summary" hidden></p>
+    </section>
+    <section id="learn" class="learning" aria-labelledby="learn-title">
+      <div class="lesson-intro"><p class="eyebrow">04 / INSIDE THE FILTER</p><h2 id="learn-title">Predict.<br>Listen.<br><em>Correct.</em></h2><p>The robot has a depth and a vertical velocity. Thrust advances its prediction. A processed depth fix pulls the estimate toward the sensor, weighted by uncertainty.</p><p>Truth is visible to you for scoring. The filter never sees it.</p><label for="question">Before a dropout, predict what will happen to the band.</label><select id="question"><option value="">Choose your prediction…</option><option value="grows">It will grow without new fixes</option><option value="shrinks">It will shrink as time passes</option><option value="same">It will stay the same</option></select><p id="answer" class="answer" aria-live="polite"></p></div>
+      <div class="lesson-card panel"><div class="panel-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">WORKED EXAMPLE / YOUR CURRENT STEP</p><h3 id="worked-title">A prior before the first fix</h3></div></div>
+        <ol class="worked"><li><span class="step-number">1</span><div><h4>Predict with thrust</h4><code>p⁻ = p + hv + ½h²u<br>v⁻ = v + hu<br>P⁻ = FPFᵀ + Q</code><p id="predict-text"></p></div></li><li><span class="step-number">2</span><div><h4>Compare the fix</h4><code>innovation = z − p⁻<br>K = P⁻Hᵀ / (P⁻₀₀ + R)</code><p id="innovation-text"></p></div></li><li><span class="step-number">3</span><div><h4>Correct the estimate</h4><code>x = x⁻ + K · innovation</code><p id="correct-text"></p></div></li></ol>
+        <p class="hint">Step advances 0.25 s; fixes arrive every 1 s. Covariance uses the Joseph update: P = (I−KH)P⁻(I−KH)ᵀ + KRKᵀ.</p>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+    <details class="model panel"><summary>Model, units &amp; limits <span>+</span></summary><div class="model-body"><p>This is a linear, one-dimensional depth experiment, positive downward. h = 0.25 s, state x = [depth in m, velocity in m/s], F = [[1,h],[0,1]], H = [1,0]. Known thrust acceleration u is applied to both world and model. Initial x = [8, 0.12], P = diag(1 m², 0.04 m²/s²).</p><p>Every step draws an independent acceleration disturbance with standard deviation 0.06 m/s², constant over that interval. Q = q [[h⁴/4,h³/2],[h³/2,h²]]. q is acceleration variance, not a standard deviation or continuous-time spectral density. The sensor slider is actual standard deviation σ; filter R is assumed variance. Nominal matched values are q = 0.0036 and R = σ².</p><p>The crosses are simulated, processed localization depth fixes every second. They are not raw sonar ranges, bearings, underwater GPS, or DVL readings. No DVL is modeled. Dead reckoning integrates only the commanded acceleration. Fix noise is independent Gaussian noise. Current and bias deliberately break the filter's assumptions; this model cannot estimate persistent bias or ocean dynamics.</p><p>The mint band is the marginal Gaussian depth interval p̂ ± 1.96√P₀₀, about 95% under a correct model. It is not a two-dimensional confidence ellipse or a guarantee. Empirical coverage can differ, especially during faults. RMSE compares positions to simulation truth and does not claim that filtering always improves accuracy.</p><p>RoboSub-inspired exploration, with gate / slalom / bin navigation as inspiration. This depth transect is a simplified teaching exercise, not an official course or scoring system.</p><p class="sources">Read further: <a href="https://www.cs.unc.edu/~welch/media/pdf/kalman_intro.pdf">Welch &amp; Bishop’s introduction</a> · <a href="https://www.bzarg.com/p/how-a-kalman-filter-works-in-pictures/">A visual filter explanation</a> · <a href="https://robonation.gitbook.io/robosub-resources/section-3-autonomy-challenge/3.2-task-descriptions">RoboSub task descriptions</a> · <a href="https://robosub.org/programs/2026/">RoboSub 2026</a></p></div></details>
+  </main>
+  <footer><span>ABYSS / A SMALL WINDOW INTO UNCERTAINTY</span><span>Local simulation · no uploads · no dependencies</span><a href="../secret/">Back to Secret ↗</a></footer>
