+    <details class="model panel"><summary>Model, units &amp; limits <span>+</span></summary><div class="model-body"><p>This is a linear, one-dimensional depth experiment, positive downward. h = 0.25 s, state x = [depth in m, velocity in m/s], F = [[1,h],[0,1]], H = [1,0]. Known thrust acceleration u is applied to both world and model. Initial x = [8, 0.12], P = diag(1 m², 0.04 m²/s²).</p><p>Every step draws an independent acceleration disturbance with standard deviation 0.06 m/s², constant over that interval. Q = q [[h⁴/4,h³/2],[h³/2,h²]]. q is acceleration variance, not a standard deviation or continuous-time spectral density. The sensor slider is actual standard deviation σ; filter R is assumed variance. Nominal matched values are q = 0.0036 and R = σ².</p><p>The crosses are simulated, processed localization depth fixes every second. They are not raw sonar ranges, bearings, underwater GPS, or DVL readings. No DVL is modeled. Dead reckoning integrates only the commanded acceleration. Fix noise is independent Gaussian noise. Current and bias deliberately break the filter's assumptions; this model cannot estimate persistent bias or ocean dynamics.</p><p>The mint band is the marginal Gaussian depth interval p̂ ± 1.96√P₀₀, about 95% under a correct model. It is not a two-dimensional confidence ellipse or a guarantee. Empirical coverage can differ, especially during faults. RMSE compares positions to simulation truth and does not claim that filtering always improves accuracy.</p><p>RoboSub-inspired exploration, with gate / slalom / bin navigation as inspiration. This depth transect is a simplified teaching exercise, not an official course or scoring system.</p><p class="sources">Read further: <a href="https://www.cs.unc.edu/~welch/media/pdf/kalman_intro.pdf">Welch &amp; Bishop’s introduction</a> · <a href="https://www.bzarg.com/p/how-a-kalman-filter-works-in-pictures/">A visual filter explanation</a> · <a href="https://robonation.gitbook.io/robosub-resources/section-3-